{
  "slug": "negacion",
  "number": 12,
  "part": 3,
  "title": "Negation",
  "tagline": "Ne… pas and its family, where each part goes, ne… que and the answer si.",
  "goal": "Say never, no longer, nothing, nobody, not yet and only; make negatives in the present, past and future, and answer si to a negative question.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "basica",
      "title": "Ne… pas",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "English uses «not» with a helper («don't», «isn't»). French has no helper but uses **two pieces** around the conjugated verb:"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "ne + verb + pas",
          "caption": "*Je {ne} parle {pas} chinois.* (I don't speak Chinese.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Before a vowel or silent h, *ne* → *n'*: *Je n'aime pas les araignées.*",
            "In the negative, *un, une, du, de la, des* → *de, d'*: *Je n'ai pas de voiture.* [[6]]"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "familia",
      "title": "The other negatives",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "They all work the same way: only the second piece changes."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Negative",
            "Meaning",
            "Example",
            "Opposite"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*ne… pas*",
              "not",
              "*Je ne danse pas.*",
              "—"
            ],
            [
              "*ne… jamais*",
              "never",
              "*Il ne mange jamais de légumes.*",
              "*toujours, souvent*"
            ],
            [
              "*ne… plus*",
              "no longer, not anymore",
              "*Je n'habite plus à Londres.*",
              "*encore, toujours*"
            ],
            [
              "*ne… rien*",
              "nothing",
              "*Elle ne voit rien.*",
              "*quelque chose*"
            ],
            [
              "*ne… personne*",
              "nobody",
              "*Je ne connais personne.*",
              "*quelqu'un*"
            ],
            [
              "*ne… pas encore*",
              "not yet",
              "*Il n'est pas encore là.*",
              "*déjà*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "In the negative, *plus* doesn't mean «more» but **«no longer»**: *Je ne mange plus de viande* = I don't eat meat anymore."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "*personne* and *rien* already contain the negative: «I don't know anybody» is *Je ne connais personne*, with no *pas*."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "posicion",
      "title": "Where each piece goes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Situation",
            "Rule",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**Present**",
              "ne + verb + pas",
              "*Je ne mange pas.*"
            ],
            [
              "**Passé composé**",
              "ne + auxiliary + pas + participle",
              "*Je n'ai pas mangé. · Il n'a rien dit.*"
            ],
            [
              "… except *personne*",
              "*personne* goes **after** the participle",
              "*Je n'ai vu personne.*"
            ],
            [
              "**Near future**",
              "ne + aller + pas + infinitive",
              "*Je ne vais pas manger.*"
            ],
            [
              "**Infinitive**",
              "*ne pas* together, in front",
              "*Attention à ne pas tomber.*"
            ],
            [
              "**With pronouns**",
              "the pronoun stays next to the verb",
              "*Je ne le vois pas.*"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sujeto",
      "title": "Rien and personne as the subject",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "They can also start the sentence. Then *ne* comes right after, and never *pas*:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Personne ne comprend.",
              "tr": "Nobody understands."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Rien n'est impossible.",
              "tr": "Nothing is impossible."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ne-que",
      "title": "Ne… que: only",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It looks negative but it isn't: *ne… que* means **only**."
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je n'ai que deux euros.",
              "tr": "I only have two euros."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Il ne mange que des pâtes.",
              "tr": "He only eats pasta."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "Since it isn't a real negative, articles **don't** change to *de*: *Il ne mange que {des} pâtes.*"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "si",
      "title": "Si: yes, to a negative question",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "Question",
            "Answering yes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*Tu aimes le chocolat ?* (positive)",
              "*Oui, j'adore !*"
            ],
            [
              "*Tu n'aimes pas le chocolat ?* (negative)",
              "*Si, j'adore !*"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "*si* is like «yes I do!» or «of course I do!» when someone assumed the opposite."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "oral",
      "title": "In conversation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In informal spoken French, the *ne* almost always disappears:"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Je sais pas.",
              "tr": "I don't know.",
              "note": "Sounds like «shay pa»"
            },
            {
              "fr": "C'est pas grave.",
              "tr": "No big deal."
            },
            {
              "fr": "J'ai rien fait.",
              "tr": "I didn't do anything."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "text": "Understand it when you hear it, but always write the *ne*."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "exercises": [
